0	\section{Experiments}
1	\label{sec:experiments}
2	
3	\subsection{Experimental Setup}
4	\label{sec:setup}
5	
6	\paragraph{Dataset.}
7	We conduct experiments on DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred}, a large-scale document-level relation extraction benchmark containing 3,053 training and 998 development documents spanning 96 relation types. Each document requires identifying relational triples across multiple sentences and grounding each triple with supporting evidence sentences. We use Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} for cross-dataset generalization evaluation.
8	
9	\paragraph{Models.}
10	Our primary model is Qwen3-4B. To study scale effects, we additionally train Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B under identical configurations. We include LLaMA-3.1-8B as a cross-family validation point.
11	
12	\paragraph{Training.}
13	All models use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}64$ and $\alpha{=}128$ over a structured output format that jointly produces relation triples and evidence sentences. For RSFT, we sample $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per document and select the top-$k{=}1$ response ranked by CED scoring (\S\ref{sec:ced}), which combines NLI-based evidence faithfulness with extraction accuracy. GRPO experiments use group sizes $G{=}4$ (QLoRA, 4-bit) and $G{=}8$ (bf16 full-precision LoRA). DPO constructs preference pairs from CED-ranked outputs.
14	
15	\paragraph{Metrics.}
16	We report five metrics. \textbf{Ign-F1} is the primary DocRED metric that ignores triples appearing in the training set, matching the official leaderboard protocol. \textbf{Rel-F1} measures relation extraction F1 on all triples. \textbf{Evi-F1 (TP-only)} measures evidence sentence F1 conditioned on correctly predicted triples, reflecting evidence quality independent of extraction errors. \textbf{Evi-F1 (joint)} includes false-positive and false-negative triples in the denominator following the DREEAM protocol~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, enabling cross-method evidence comparison. \textbf{EDCR} (Evidence-Decoupling Ratio) is our proposed diagnostic: the ratio of evidence recall on correctly-predicted triples to overall extraction recall, where values substantially exceeding the SFT baseline indicate RL-induced decoupling. All primary results use the development set; test set submission is pending.
17	
18	
19	\subsection{Main Results}
20	\label{sec:main_results}
21	
22	Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison between SFT, no-evidence SFT, and RSFT CED on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline achieves 39.74 Ign-F1 with joint evidence generation. Removing evidence from the output format entirely (no-evidence SFT) improves extraction to 47.21 Ign-F1, a 7.47-point gap that quantifies the \emph{evidence tax} at the 4B scale. RSFT CED achieves a four-seed mean of 44.66 Ign-F1 (+4.92 over SFT, standard deviation 0.49) while maintaining evidence quality (Evi-F1 81.22 vs.\ 79.86 for SFT). The EDCR decreases from 0.6982 to 0.6498, confirming tighter coupling between extraction and evidence capabilities. Individual seeds (42, 43, 44, 45) yield Ign-F1 of 44.23, 45.12, 45.11, and 44.16 respectively, confirming low variance across runs.
23	
24	The remaining gap to the no-evidence upper bound (2.09 points) represents the residual cost of producing structured evidence, a cost that shrinks at larger scales as we show in Section~\ref{sec:scaling}.
25	
26	\begin{table}[t]
27	\centering
28	\caption{Main results on DocRED dev set (Qwen3-4B). Four-seed mean is the primary result (std 0.49). Individual seeds shown for transparency. Best Ign-F1 in \textbf{bold}; best among evidence-generating methods \underline{underlined}.}
29	\label{tab:main}
30	\small
31	\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
32	\toprule
33	\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Ign-F1} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} \\
34	\midrule
35	SFT baseline & 39.74 & 0.4049 & 79.86 & 0.6982 \\
36	SFT no-evidence & \textbf{47.21} & \textbf{0.4876} & -- & -- \\
37	\midrule
38	RSFT CED (s42) & 44.23 & 0.4517 & 81.75 & 0.6569 \\
39	RSFT CED (s43) & \underline{45.12} & \underline{0.4601} & 80.87 & 0.6521 \\
40	RSFT CED (s44) & 45.11 & 0.4621 & 80.50 & 0.6350 \\
41	RSFT CED (s45) & 44.16 & 0.4514 & 81.74 & 0.6550 \\
42	\midrule
43	RSFT CED (mean) & 44.66 & 0.4563 & 81.22 & 0.6498 \\
44	\bottomrule
45	\end{tabular}
46	\end{table}
47	
48	
49	\subsection{Evidence-Extraction Decoupling Spectrum}
50	\label{sec:spectrum}
51	
52	Different RL algorithms produce qualitatively different failure modes when applied to joint evidence-and-extraction generation. We organize these outcomes along a \emph{decoupling spectrum} ranging from catastrophic collapse to mild perturbation, shown in Table~\ref{tab:spectrum} and Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}.
53	
54	\paragraph{GRPO QLoRA: catastrophic collapse.}
55	Under QLoRA quantization ($G{=}4$), GRPO drives Rel-F1 to 0.0138 (near zero extraction) while Evi-F1 rises to 85.04 and EDCR reaches 0.9876. The model learns to produce well-formed evidence sentences but loses the ability to predict correct relation triples. This extreme decoupling occurs because 4-bit quantization constrains the effective parameter space, forcing the reward signal to optimize whichever sub-task yields easier gradient progress. Evidence sentence generation, being closer to extractive summarization, wins this competition.
56	
57	\paragraph{GRPO bf16: mild, non-monotonic.}
58	Full-precision GRPO ($G{=}8$) avoids catastrophe. Its training trajectory shows non-monotonic Rel-F1 fluctuation (0.434 at step 30, 0.428 at step 50, 0.434 at step 70, 0.455 at step 100), stabilizing near the SFT baseline. The best checkpoint achieves Rel-F1 0.4549 with Evi-F1 80.86 and EDCR 0.6318. The decoupling is mild: bf16 precision preserves enough capacity for both sub-tasks to improve jointly, though the non-monotonic trajectory suggests fragile optimization dynamics that require careful checkpoint selection.
59	
60	\paragraph{DPO: gradual decay.}
61	DPO preference learning shows a progressive pattern. At 1 epoch, Rel-F1 remains at 0.4034 with Evi-F1 80.88 and EDCR 0.7139. Extended training (3 epochs) degrades extraction to 0.3429 while evidence improves to 84.03\footnote{DPO 3-epoch results use the pre-D076 evaluation protocol; included for trend analysis.}, with EDCR rising to 0.7294. Unlike GRPO's sudden collapse, DPO gradually trades extraction quality for evidence quality as training progresses.
62	
63	\paragraph{RSFT: no decoupling.}
64	RSFT avoids the decoupling problem entirely. By selecting high-quality complete outputs (jointly good extraction and evidence) rather than applying gradient-based reward maximization, RSFT maintains both capabilities simultaneously (mean Rel-F1 0.4563, Evi-F1 81.22, EDCR 0.6498). The key insight is that rejection sampling operates on discrete outputs rather than differentiable reward signals, so it cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find.
65	
66	\begin{table}[t]
67	\centering
68	\caption{Evidence-extraction decoupling across RL paradigms (Qwen3-4B). Severity indicates the degree of extraction collapse relative to evidence improvement.}
69	\label{tab:spectrum}
70	\small
71	\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
72	\toprule
73	\textbf{Paradigm} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} & \textbf{Severity} \\
74	\midrule
75	GRPO & QLoRA $G{=}4$ & 0.0138 & 85.04 & 0.9876 & Catastrophic \\
76	GRPO & bf16 $G{=}8$ & 0.4549 & 80.86 & 0.6318 & Mild \\
77	DPO & bf16 1ep & 0.4034 & 80.88 & 0.7139 & Gradual \\
78	DPO & bf16 3ep$^\dagger$ & 0.3429 & 84.03 & 0.7294 & Gradual \\
79	\midrule
80	SFT & baseline & 0.4049 & 79.86 & 0.6982 & None \\
81	RSFT & CED (mean) & 0.4563 & 81.22 & 0.6498 & None \\
82	\bottomrule
83	\end{tabular}
84	\vspace{-2mm}
85	{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Pre-D076 evaluation protocol.\par}
86	\end{table}
87	
88	\begin{figure}[t]
89	\centering
90	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_spectrum.pdf}
91	\caption{Decoupling spectrum visualization. Each method is plotted by Rel-F1 (x-axis) and Evi-F1 (y-axis). Arrows indicate training progression for DPO (1ep$\to$3ep) and GRPO bf16 trajectory. Ideal methods occupy the upper-right quadrant; decoupled methods drift toward the upper-left.}
92	\label{fig:spectrum}
93	\end{figure}
94	
95	
96	\subsection{Scale-Dependent Evidence Tax}
97	\label{sec:scaling}
98	
99	The evidence tax, defined as the Ign-F1 difference between no-evidence and joint-evidence SFT, varies non-monotonically across model scales. Table~\ref{tab:scaling} and Figure~\ref{fig:scaling} present this analysis across four models.
100	
101	At the 4B scale, the evidence tax is severe: $-7.47$ points. Generating evidence sentences alongside relation triples consumes capacity that would otherwise improve extraction. At 8B, the tax nearly vanishes ($-0.28$ points for Qwen3-8B, $-0.68$ for LLaMA-3.1-8B), indicating that 8B-class models have sufficient capacity to handle both tasks simultaneously. The most surprising result appears at 1.7B: instead of paying a larger tax, the smallest model \emph{benefits} from evidence generation (+8.04 points). On a fair evaluation subset that controls for output format effects (see Appendix), this benefit is +6.2 points. We hypothesize that for small models, the evidence generation task provides implicit regularization, forcing the model to attend to supporting context rather than memorizing surface-level patterns.
102	
103	RSFT improves all scales. The gain is +2.64 for 1.7B, +4.92 for 4B, and +3.82 for 8B, confirming that CED-based selection generalizes across model capacities. The 4B model benefits most because it has the largest evidence tax to recover from. A striking consequence of this pattern is that 1.7B RSFT achieves 50.50 Ign-F1, nearly matching 8B SFT at 50.51. CED-based rejection sampling enables a 1.7B model to rival a 4.7$\times$ larger model on evidence-grounded extraction, with direct implications for deployment cost: practitioners can substitute a small model with RSFT for a large model with vanilla SFT at comparable accuracy.
104	
105	\begin{table}[t]
106	\centering
107	\caption{Scale-dependent evidence tax and RSFT gain. Evidence tax = SFT with evidence minus SFT no-evidence (positive means evidence helps). All results on DocRED dev set Ign-F1.}
108	\label{tab:scaling}
109	\small
110	\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
111	\toprule
112	\textbf{Model} & \textbf{SFT w/ evi} & \textbf{No-evi} & \textbf{RSFT} & \textbf{Evi Tax} & \textbf{RSFT Gain} \\
113	\midrule
114	Qwen3-1.7B & 47.86 & 39.82 & 50.50 & +8.04 & +2.64 \\
115	Qwen3-4B & 39.74 & 47.21 & 44.66 & $-$7.47 & +4.92 \\
116	Qwen3-8B & 50.51 & 50.79 & \textbf{54.33} & $-$0.28 & +3.82 \\
117	LLaMA-3.1-8B & 53.84 & 54.52 & 55.58 & $-$0.68 & +1.74 \\
118	\bottomrule
119	\end{tabular}
120	\end{table}
121	
122	\begin{figure}[t]
123	\centering
124	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_scaling.pdf}
125	\caption{Evidence tax across model scales. Bar heights show Ign-F1 for each training configuration. The gap between no-evidence and SFT bars reverses direction between 1.7B and 4B, revealing the non-monotonic nature of the evidence tax. RSFT consistently narrows the gap toward the no-evidence upper bound.}
126	\label{fig:scaling}
127	\end{figure}
128	
129	
130	\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
131	\label{sec:evidence_strategy}
132	
133	We compare joint generation against post-hoc retrieval, using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam} for fair cross-strategy comparison. Post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$ sentences per triple) achieves higher Rel-F1 (0.4876) by avoiding the evidence tax, but produces lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 0.347 with EDCR 0.766. RSFT CED (s43) reaches Evi-F1 (joint) 0.361 with EDCR 0.654, a 1.4-point evidence advantage and 0.112 EDCR reduction. Joint training produces more faithful evidence grounded in the extraction reasoning rather than retrieved post-hoc.
134	
135	
136	\subsection{Generalization}
137	\label{sec:generalization}
138	
139	We evaluate generalization along three axes: cross-family transfer, cross-dataset robustness, and comparison to specialized supervised methods.
140	
141	\paragraph{Cross-family transfer.}
142	LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the scaling patterns observed with Qwen3. Its SFT baseline achieves 53.84 Ign-F1 (Rel-F1 0.5514, Evi-F1 81.51), and the no-evidence variant reaches 54.52 Ign-F1 (Rel-F1 0.5624). The evidence tax of $-0.68$ points mirrors the near-zero tax seen for Qwen3-8B ($-0.28$), supporting our claim that 8B-class models absorb the cost of joint evidence generation regardless of architecture family. RSFT CED improves LLaMA to 55.58 Ign-F1 (Rel-F1 0.5674, Evi-F1 82.14, EDCR 0.6128), a +1.74 point gain consistent with the pattern at the 8B scale (Qwen3-8B: +3.82). A second seed (s43: 56.19 Ign-F1, EDCR 0.6073) confirms robustness.
143	
144	\paragraph{Cross-dataset robustness.}
145	On Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, our best Qwen3-4B RSFT model shows an Ign-F1 drop of 3.15 points compared to DocRED dev set performance, consistent with the domain shift expected from re-annotation. Critically, Evi-F1 remains at 81.7, within 0.5 points of the DocRED result. The evidence extraction capability transfers robustly even when relation extraction degrades, further supporting the partial independence between the two capabilities that EDCR measures.
146	
147	\paragraph{Comparison to supervised methods.}
148	Our best joint Evi-F1 of approximately 0.36 (using the DREEAM-protocol denominator) falls below the supervised DREEAM result of 54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}. This gap stems primarily from extraction performance rather than evidence quality: our TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%, indicating that when the model correctly identifies a triple, it produces high-quality evidence. The overall joint Evi-F1 is diluted by false-positive and false-negative triples in the denominator. Closing this gap requires improving the base extraction capability of LLM-based methods, an orthogonal direction to the evidence faithfulness problem we address. Our contribution is demonstrating that LLMs can generate grounded evidence jointly with extraction, and that RSFT maintains this capability under optimization pressure where RL methods fail.
149

