9	
10	
11	\subsection{Supervised Fine-Tuning Baseline}
12	\label{sec:sft}
13	
14	We fine-tune all models with LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} at rank $r{=}64$ and scaling factor $\alpha{=}128$, applied to attention projection matrices. The training data consists of DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred} training documents converted to the structured output format described above, where each example maps a document and its entity set to the complete set of relation triples with evidence annotations. We also train a \emph{no-evidence} variant under identical configuration but with evidence references removed from the output format, producing only \texttt{(head, relation, tail)}. This variant serves as an upper bound on extraction performance by eliminating the generation cost of evidence, and allows direct measurement of the evidence tax at each model scale. Detailed hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, number of epochs) are reported in the Appendix.
15	
16	
17	\subsection{Optimization Paradigms}
18	\label{sec:paradigms}
19	
20	We evaluate three post-SFT optimization paradigms that differ in how they incorporate quality signals from the CED scoring function (\S\ref{sec:ced}).
21	
22	\paragraph{GRPO.}
23	Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} is an online RL method that eliminates the need for a separate value network by computing advantages within groups of sampled outputs. For each training document, the policy generates $G$ candidate responses, which are scored by CED. The advantage of each response is computed relative to the group mean, and the policy is updated to increase the probability of above-average outputs. We test two configurations: QLoRA with $G{=}4$ groups under 4-bit quantization, and bf16 full-precision LoRA with $G{=}8$. Because GRPO applies gradient updates that directly optimize the reward signal, evidence quality and extraction accuracy compete for gradient capacity. We analyze this multi-objective tension in Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}.
24	
25	\paragraph{DPO.}
26	Direct Preference Optimization~\citep{rafailov2023direct} reformulates RLHF as a classification problem over preference pairs, without explicit reward models. We construct preference data from the SFT model's sampled outputs: for each document, the response with the highest CED score is labeled as preferred and the lowest-scoring response as rejected. DPO then adjusts the policy to increase the log-probability ratio between preferred and rejected completions. We train for 1 and 3 epochs to observe the temporal dynamics of evidence-extraction trade-offs under implicit optimization.
27	
28	\paragraph{RSFT.}
29	Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning is a single-round variant of iterative self-improvement methods such as STaR~\citep{zelikman2022star}. The procedure is straightforward: the SFT model generates $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per training document, each candidate is scored by CED, and only the top-$k{=}1$ response is retained as training data. From 24,216 candidate outputs, this selection yields 9,042 training examples (37.3\% acceptance rate). Unlike GRPO and DPO, RSFT does not apply gradient-based optimization pressure toward a reward signal. Instead, it improves training data quality through filtering: the model trains on its own best outputs rather than all outputs. This distinction is critical because discrete selection over complete outputs cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find. The contribution of RSFT in our setting is not the framework itself, which is well-established, but its application to evidence-grounded DocRE with CED as a domain-specific quality filter.
30	
31	
32	\subsection{Contrastive Evidence Discrimination}
33	\label{sec:ced}
34	
35	CED is a scoring function that evaluates the faithfulness of predicted evidence sentences to their associated relation triples. It builds on Natural Language Inference (NLI) to measure whether evidence actually entails the predicted relation, rather than merely co-occurring with the relevant entities.
36	
37	For a predicted triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$ with evidence sentences $E$, CED constructs a positive NLI pair: the premise is the concatenation of sentences in $E$, and the hypothesis is a natural-language verbalization of the relation (e.g., ``$e_h$ has relation $r$ with $e_t$''). An NLI model (\texttt{cross-encoder/nli-deberta-v3-base}) produces an entailment probability $p^+$ for this pair. The contrastive component introduces hard negatives drawn from the same document. For each triple, we collect sentences that mention $e_h$ or $e_t$ but are not in the predicted evidence set. These entity-sharing distractors serve as negative premises, and the NLI model scores each to obtain $p^-_1, \ldots, p^-_m$. The CED score for a single triple is:
38	\begin{equation}
39	\text{CED}(e_h, r, e_t, E) = p^+ - \max_j \, p^-_j
40	\label{eq:ced}
41	\end{equation}
42	This formulation forces the scoring function to distinguish sentences that genuinely support the relation from sentences that merely mention the same entities. A high CED score indicates that the evidence provides relation-specific support beyond entity co-occurrence.
43	
44	The document-level CED score aggregates over all predicted triples, weighted by extraction correctness: triples that match ground-truth relations contribute their CED scores, while incorrect triples receive zero credit. This coupling ensures that CED rewards both faithful evidence and accurate extraction jointly.
45	
46	We also evaluate a \emph{flat-NLI} baseline that uses only the positive entailment score $p^+$ without the contrastive term. In our experiments (Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}), CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT outcomes (mean Rel-F1 difference of 0.02pp across available seeds), and both outperform random selection and no-scoring baselines. We adopt CED as the default because the contrastive formulation provides interpretable per-triple discrimination scores that flat-NLI lacks.
47	
48	
49	\subsection{Evidence-Decoupling Ratio}
50	\label{sec:edcr}
51	
52	We introduce the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) as a diagnostic metric that quantifies the degree of misalignment between a model's extraction and evidence capabilities. EDCR is not a training component; it serves purely as an analytical tool for comparing optimization methods.
53	
54	EDCR is defined as:
55	\begin{equation}
56	\text{EDCR} = \frac{\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}}{\text{Rel-Recall}}
57	\label{eq:edcr}
58	\end{equation}
59	where $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ is evidence sentence recall computed only over correctly predicted triples (true positives), and $\text{Rel-Recall}$ is overall relation extraction recall. The numerator captures evidence quality on the subset of triples the model gets right; the denominator captures how many triples the model identifies in total.
60	
61	The intuition is as follows. A model that correctly identifies a few triples and provides excellent evidence for them, but misses most triples entirely, will have high $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ and low $\text{Rel-Recall}$, yielding a high EDCR. This pattern signals that evidence quality has decoupled from extraction capability: the model produces faithful evidence only within a narrow comfort zone.
62	
63	EDCR operates at two diagnostic levels. At the method level, comparing EDCR across optimization paradigms reveals a strong negative correlation with Rel-F1 (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.965$): methods with higher EDCR produce worse extraction, confirming that elevated EDCR signals problematic decoupling. At the per-relation level, computing EDCR for individual relation types reveals variation that is independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.064$, $p{=}0.58$). This independence means EDCR captures relation-specific extraction-evidence misalignment that aggregate evidence metrics miss. The SFT baseline EDCR (0.6982) reflects the inherent cost of evidence generation; EDCR values substantially exceeding this level (e.g., GRPO QLoRA at 0.9876) indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains, while values below the baseline (e.g., RSFT at 0.6521) reflect improved co-optimization.
